//OUT="7"

// 33_a_continue_does_not_make_a_while_true_fall_through.cpl with the `cnt` one
// loop further in and carrying a level. The `2` reaches past the `for` and back
// to the top of the `while true` — it restarts that loop rather than leaving it,
// so the code after the loop is still unreachable and no `ret` is needed there.
//
// This is the mirror of 83e_a_levelled_break_escapes_a_while_true.cpl:
// the same shape, the same level, and the opposite answer, because `brk 2` exits
// the outer loop and `cnt 2` does not. A check that resolved levels but then
// treated both keywords as exits would reject this and demand an unreachable
// return.

fn f n
    i = 0
    while true
        i++
        for j 0..3
            if i % 2 == 0
                cnt 2
        if i * i > n
            ret i

fn main
    out "{f(30)}"
